Z||000000||qwikgta||01-12-2001||11:16 PM||||Im sure that this is a hell of a question but...I have not committed yet to a engine swap..  I plan to swap out my 2.5L in favor of either a 3.4 DOHC a 3.8 SC or a V8.  Which one is the easier to install, I know the perf diff, this is not my question.  My interest is $$$ and time.  My car is a 88 coupe and I am not sure which swap is the easier.  Was the 88 cradle set up for the 3.4. If so will the stock motor mounts for the 3.4 bolt right up.  With an engine swap does the motor and tranie shift position in the bay or will the engine bolt up to the tranie with the trans in the same spot.  In other words do I need to get coustom trans mounts.  I understand that a swap is not for the weekend wrench turner, I have done them before just not in a Fiero yet.  Also, what items do I need from the donor car, I know the big items but what do you do about the power steering pump/pully and what if you don't want A/C.  If there are some good web sites that answer these questions just let me know.  Thanks<P>------------------<BR>QWIKGTA<BR>88 T-Top, 63K miles<BR>http://robsfieroproject.homestead.com/index.html||||reg||1
Z||000001||tgowens||01-12-2001||11:54 PM||||power steering??? I work on Fieros all day every day and have yet to see one with power steering.<P>My opinion is: the 3.4 RWD is the easiest swap for the $$$ - not the 3.4 FWD (which is the Dual Overhead Cam) - the 3.4L from the '93-'95 Fierbird and Camaro is the easiest swap probably followed by the 4.9 Cadillac engine. With all others you are going to be doing more fabrication and adapting than with any of the two I mentioned. The 2.5 to 3.4 (Rear Wheel Drive only) in an '88 coupe won't be too difficult but you will have to modify certain things (like coolant lines, etc.......<P>But, you asked what was the easiest and lowest $$$ - that's just my opinion.........||||reg||1
Z||000002||mrfiero||01-13-2001||12:04 AM||||Terry,<P>I think he means what to do about the power steering pump from the donor engine (like the 3800).<P>That said, I agree that the regular 3.4 V-6 is the easiest swap.  The DOHC 3.4 is more involved, but not too bad.<P>The '88 coupe shares the same cradle as the Formula & GT (although auto. coupes have an additional tranny mount bracket), so the V-6 will bolt in with the proper mount brackets. ||||reg||1
Z||000003||qwikgta||01-15-2001||10:53 AM||||I guess that shows my ingnorance, I assumed that the engines had power steering pumps, like RWD engines and I was not sure what to do with it if it came with the engine.  Since my engine is the 2.5 and not the 2.8 am I going to have to buy all the other parts for the 3.4   All the other threads that talk about this swap mention that you have to bolt on the original 2.8 parts.  Since I do not have a 2.8 am I going to have to buy them.  Also, I have not read a lot about the 4.9 engine. This is not the northstar correct..  Is there a good site to read more about the 4.9 swap... and what year/car did they they come in.  Thanks<P>Rob<P>------------------<BR>QWIKGTA<BR>88 T-Top, 63K miles<BR>http://robsfieroproject.homestead.com/index.html||||reg||1
